---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>

  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A habit loop that sticks is usually four plain steps: a cue that starts the action, a craving
      for the outcome, a response that is tiny enough to do today, and a reward that makes the loop
      feel worth repeating. Identity language helps: act like the kind of person who already does
      this, then let the streak be evidence, not a scoreboard.
    </p>
    <p>
      This browser habit streak tracker keeps that loop on your device. You check a habit once per
      local calendar day, see an honest current streak, keep your longest streak after a miss, and
      read warm recovery copy instead of guilt. Nothing is uploaded, and there is no account.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Habit Streak Tracker →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="habit-loop">
    <h2>The Habit Loop in Plain Language <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Popular habit writing (including ideas associated with Atomic Habits) describes a cycle most
      people already feel. This guide uses that educational framing only. It is not an official
      product, and it does not claim affiliation with any book.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Cue.</strong> Something obvious that says now. After I pour coffee. When I sit at the
      desk. A cue you can see beats a vague hope that you will remember.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Craving.</strong> The feeling you want: calm, clean teeth, a clear inbox, being
      someone who keeps promises to yourself. Craving is the motive; the checkmark is not the
      motive.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Response.</strong> The action itself, made easy. Two minutes of stretching counts.
      One page of reading counts. The two-minute rule exists because a tiny response still closes
      the loop, and a closed loop is easier to repeat tomorrow.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reward.</strong> Something satisfying right away: the streak bump, a calm breath, the
      sense that you showed up. Delayed life benefits matter, but the brain needs a near-term
      signal that the loop worked.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Make it obvious, easy, and satisfying">
      <p>
        If the cue is invisible, the action is huge, or the reward never shows up, the loop frays.
        Shrink the response first. Add a clear cue second. Let the tracker handle a light, honest
        reward third.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="tiny-habits">
    <h2>Tiny Habits Beat Hero Days <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      People fail habit trackers when the first version of the habit is too big. Meditate thirty
      minutes is a project. Sit and breathe for two minutes is a habit you can protect on a bad
      day. Start with the version you can still do when you are tired.
    </p>
    <p>
      Habit stacking is a practical cue: After I ___, I will ___. Pair the new action with
      something you already do. After I brush my teeth, I will floss one tooth. After I open the
      laptop, I will write one sentence. The stack note on each habit is optional, but it turns a
      name into a next action.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="identity">
    <h2>Identity Over Outcome <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Outcome goals sound like I want to read more. Identity goals sound like I am a reader. Each
      check is a vote for that identity. You do not need a perfect month; you need evidence that
      the story you tell about yourself is becoming true.
    </p>
    <p>
      Optional identity lines on this tool are short prompts such as I am someone who moves daily.
      Use them when they help. Skip them when the name alone is enough.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="honest-streaks">
    <h2>Honest Streaks and Never Miss Twice <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A streak is consecutive local calendar days with a check. Miss a day and the current streak
      resets to zero. The longest streak stays, so one gap does not erase your history. There are
      no grace days or skip tokens here on purpose: honesty beats a streak that quietly cheats.
    </p>
    <p>
      All-or-nothing thinking is the trap. One miss is data, not a verdict. Never miss twice means
      the recovery move is to show up today. The tracker keeps encouraging copy and your longest
      streak visible so a reset does not feel like starting from nothing.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Guilt is not a feature">
      <p>
        Cloud habit apps often mix streaks with XP, leaderboards, and shame after a break. A
        private checklist should encourage the next check, not punish the last miss.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-use">
    <h2>How to Use This Tracker <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Start with one to three tiny habits from the starter chips, or add your own. Keep the active
      list at eight or fewer so Today stays scannable. Check once per local day; tap again the same
      day to undo. Expand a habit for its heat grid, identity, and stack notes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Export JSON before you clear site data or switch browsers. Habits do not sync across devices.
      Archive habits you are pausing; delete only when you want that history gone.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Track today on your device →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Privacy <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded. Checks, names, and notes live in browser
      storage on this profile. Clearing site data deletes them unless you exported a backup first.
    </p>
  </section>

</GuideLayout>
